## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

---

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `Object.is` to compare props. Primitives compare by value, but **objects and functions compare by reference** — a new reference every render means a new prop, even if the content is identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child every time Parent renders, even if nothing changed
function Parent() {
  return <Child config={{ theme: 'dark' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

Each render creates a new object literal `{}` and a new arrow function `() => ...`, so Child always sees "new" props.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component; skips re-render if props are shallowly equal.
Use it when a component is expensive to render and its parent re-renders often for unrelated reasons.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ config, onClick }) => { /* ... */ });
```

**`useCallback`** — memoizes a function reference across renders.
Use it when passing a callback to a memoized child (otherwise memo does nothing).

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(), []);
```

**`useMemo`** — memoizes a computed value or object reference.
Use it for expensive calculations, or to stabilize an object/array passed as a prop.

```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark' }), []);
```

**When NOT to use them:** don't wrap everything — memoization has its own cost (cache comparison on every render). Only apply when you have a measured problem or a clear structural reason.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

Every consumer re-renders when the context **value reference** changes. The most common mistake is constructing the value inline:

```jsx
// Every Parent render creates a new object → all consumers re-render
<MyContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
```

Fix: stabilize the value with `useMemo`.

```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user, setUser]);
<MyContext.Provider value={value}>
```

A deeper fix: **split contexts** — a read context and a dispatch context. Dispatch never changes, so components that only call actions never re-render on state changes.

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before** — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Dashboard` does, even though its data hasn't changed.

```jsx
function Dashboard({ userId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const items = fetchItems(userId); // returns a new array reference each time

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList
        items={items}
        onSelect={(id) => console.log(id)}  // new function every render
      />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* slow render */ }
```

**After** — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes.

```jsx
function Dashboard({ userId }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  // Stable reference: only recomputes when userId changes
  const items = useMemo(() => fetchItems(userId), [userId]);

  // Stable reference: doesn't change between renders
  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => console.log(id), []);

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Clicked {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={items} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

// Skips render entirely if props are shallowly equal
const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* slow render */
});
```

**Key insight:** `React.memo` alone does nothing if the props are unstable references. The three tools work as a system: `memo` on the component + `useMemo`/`useCallback` on the values passed to it.
